level10@SnowCrash:~$ ltrace ./level10 /tmp/asd.txt 127.0.0.1
access("/tmp/asd.txt", 4)                                   = 0
connect(3, 0xbffff71c, 16, 0, 0)                            = 0
open("/tmp/asd.txt", 0, 010)                                = 4
read(4, "salut\n", 4096)                                    = 6
write(3, "salut\n", 6)                                      = 6
puts("wrote file!"wrote file!)                              = 12

Via ltrace on remarque un TOUCTOU => access -> connect -> open

access vérifie les droits du fichier 	-> OK pour asd.txt mais pas pour token
connect lance la connection TCP			-> LENT
open ouvre le fichier puis le lit		-> valeur du TOKEN

A program is vulnerable to a TOCTOU race condition if it:
    Checks some property or validates some data, and then
    Takes some action based on this information;

and the following are the case:
    It is possible for other programs that run concurrently with this program to execute in between steps 1 and 2 (the operation is not atomic), and
    Other programs can change the property or data (existence of outside control).

Then, if another process does in fact change the property between step 1 and 2, step 2 is performed based on outdated information, which can have unintended consequences.

=> Trouver un moyen de refaire la même chose mais swap de fichier pendant que connect est en cours

Séquence:

0->	nc -lvk 6969	-> k permet de garder la connection ouverte apres l'ecriture
1->	access sur asd.txt avec les droits
2->	connect prend son temps
3=>	changer l'arg par ./token		-> swap ? X -> Lien symbolique ? A tester	=> ln -s /tmp/asd.txt /home/user/level10/token
4-> open, read & write sur ./token

Exemple d'attaque wiki:

Victim		-> access(file)
Attacker	-> symlink("/etc/passwd", "file")
Victim		-> open("file", O_WRONLY); write(fd, buffer, sizeof(buffer));

an attacker can exploit the race condition between the access and open to trick the setuid victim into overwriting an entry in the system password database.
TOCTOU races can be used for privilege escalation to get administrative access to a machine.

Although this sequence of events requires precise timing,
it is possible for an attacker to arrange such conditions without too much difficulty.

Exploiting a TOCTOU race condition requires precise timing to ensure that the attacker's operations interleave properly with the victim's.
In the example above, the attacker must execute the symlink system call precisely between the access and open.

In the case of BSD 4.3 mail utility and mktemp(),
the attacker can simply keep launching mail utility in one process,
and keep guessing the temporary file names and keep making symlinks in another process.
The attack can usually succeed in less than one minute.

===> Faire tourner en boucle un script qui creer les liens symbolique entre asd.txt et token puis exec jusqu'à avoir le bon timing

